#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38c34ab-8ac6-5dd1-9812-e1a446e77ebf" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "fef2e81e-33db-518d-91ad-a3d39637d736" :question/math-academy-id "e-10747" :question/problem "Represent the solution of the inequality $14 \\lt 5x-6$ on a number line." :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}14 & < 5x - 6 \\\\ 14 + 6 & < 5x - 6 + 6 \\\\ 20 & < 5x + 0 \\\\ 20 & < 5x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20 & < 5x \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & < \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & < \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ 4 & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember that we also need to flip the inequality.\n$x \\gt 4$\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\gt 4$. The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b6c834b9f69e2610a3c61bb765ec0cba5ad03cfcd22a3ddb0290ff0fa3882f0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78151f63-63dc-5338-bf9b-90f3fd825173" :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Inequalities When Flipping Is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "56743b65-6e81-557e-a0b6-e04dbe9fc7e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10806" :question/problem "Represent the solution of the inequality $-6a > -12$ on a number line." :question/worked-solution "The variable $a$ is being *multiplied* by $-6$. To isolate $a$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-6$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-6$.\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6a & >- 12 \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6} & <\\frac{-12}{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6} & <2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-6a}{-6} & < 2 \\\\ \\frac{-6a}{-6} & < 2 \\\\ a & < 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a< 2$. The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5947166eb517c08355c09c265f6a2c1f87bdafd776b2e03f5503585568b90b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9415209a-a06f-5d07-900f-7deb280837b5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "5280bed5-8ca3-55e5-9a6b-af86bf858e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10945" :question/problem "Represent the solution of the inequality $k -12 \\lt -5$ on a number line." :question/worked-solution "To isolate $k$, we need to perform the *opposite* of *subtracting* $12$, which is *adding* $12$.\nRemember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k - 12 & < - 5 \\\\ k - 12 + 12 & < - 5 + 12 \\\\ k + 0 & < 7 \\\\ k & < 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $k \\lt 7$. The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9c/9cc25f8eec7586ce65a17be3eb7af6ee509d014bf8dc9896cd45c6ef99769250.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9415209a-a06f-5d07-900f-7deb280837b5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91dc0cc7-bdc2-55b3-bc63-cb9b07e57890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/bae522d2b6f5d1ddf58bfad76ca18d1e6057089bdc9063e7df4bad19a6339992.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190631} {:answer/id #uuid "d125d33e-4ad5-5261-80ed-9eca6ff4e950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.ty